#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368088 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221434 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1dd5c036-7a05-5ca4-aae9-1cf585839ebb" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the First Shifting Theorem and Linearity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71126cdc-4db1-5d85-972e-cd3f7216926d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{5}{(s-1)^{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368090} {:answer/id #uuid "1f938eca-5b97-5407-a40f-c988b2a16b67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{120}{(s-1)^{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368091} {:answer/id #uuid "9820ac7b-3cc2-5748-8cbc-e6f3462796e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{24}{(s-1)^{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368092} {:answer/id #uuid "8afdd9ac-60ed-53f4-9d08-0c4a351de9b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{120}{(s-1)^{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368093} {:answer/id #uuid "37dc610f-9fa4-543d-8aae-3cf583648376" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{120}{(s-1)^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368094}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8afdd9ac-60ed-53f4-9d08-0c4a351de9b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{120}{(s-1)^{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368093} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ff80550-7623-5ff1-866b-cae616e84c57" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368089}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7b5c8893-14eb-5d02-a313-3aa760c297c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-332128" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{n}} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nuse linearity of Laplace transforms and the first shifting theorem to find the Laplace transform of $f(t)=7{t}^{2}+5{t}^{4}{e}^{t}$ for $s>1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the linearity properties of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{7{t}^{2}+5{t}^{4}{e}^{t}} \\\\  & =L{7{t}^{2}}+L{5{t}^{4}{e}^{t}} \\\\  & =7L{{t}^{2}}+5L{{t}^{4}{e}^{t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that, for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{n}} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{2}} & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}} \\\\ L{{t}^{4}} & =\\frac{24}{{s}^{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first shifting theorem states that if $L{f(t)}=F(s),$ then $L{{e}^{at}f(t)}=F(s-a).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nL{{t}^{4}{e}^{t}}=\\frac{24}{(s-1)^{5}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =7L{{t}^{2}}+5L{{t}^{4}{e}^{t}} \\\\  & =7\\cdot \\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+5\\cdot \\frac{24}{(s-1)^{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{120}{(s-1)^{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis transform is valid for $s>1.$"}]}
